  "worked_solution": "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration. \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}6{x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7{x}^{6/7}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{6}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{1}^{6}}-7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{a}^{6}}] \\\\  & =7(1)-7(0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
